<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Traidenis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1282">1282</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Traidenis was grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from about 1269 to 1282, a pagan ruler who restored the power of the state after the turmoil following the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>. He fought the Livonian Order and the princes of Galicia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, and supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> and Prussians against the crusading orders. His reign prepared the way for the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytenis">Vytenis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/traidenis.jpg" alt="Traidenis" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Guagnini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lithuanian_Grand_Duke_Traidenis.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His ancestry is unknown, but Traidenis came from Aukštaitija and was Duke of Kernavė before he took the throne. After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> was assassinated in 1263, his son Vaišelga, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monk who had set aside his vows, ruled with the support of the princes of Halych-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and shared power with Vasylko Romanovych and Shvarn of Halych. Traidenis saw a ruler leaning on Ruthenian Orthodox arms as a threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s polytheistic traditions and pressed his own claim.</p><p>Vaišelga abdicated in favour of Shvarn and returned to monastic life, and in late 1267 he was murdered by Leo, Shvarn's brother. Whether Traidenis conspired with Leo is uncertain. Vasylko fought on alone through 1268 and was defeated; Traidenis lost three brothers, Barza, Liesis and Svalkenis, in the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tension with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> led to the war of 1274–1276, which Traidenis won despite Mongol support for his enemies, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-ruthenia">Black Ruthenia</a>, with Novogrudok, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> and Slonim, firmly into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. He raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> and Łęczyca but spared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, allying with its Piast dukes by marriage: in 1279 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-of-masovia">Bolesław II of Masovia</a> married his daughter Gaudemunda.</p><p>Against the Livonian Order he won the Battle of Karuse on the ice near Saaremaa in 1270, killing the master Otto von Lutterberg, and later killed his successor Andreas von Westfalen. The Order built Dinaburg Castle on his lands in 1273, which he failed to take. In 1279 the Order's army was defeated at the Battle of Aizkraukle and its master Ernst von Rassburg killed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nameisis">Nameisis</a> rose and sought his help. In 1281 he took Jersika Castle and exchanged it for Dinaburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Traidenis died soon afterwards, the first known Lithuanian monarch to die a natural death, and aid to the exhausted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> dwindled. He left a state more unified than under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>, with firm royal authority over a core of ethnic Lithuanian lands, and he sheltered Prussian and Skalvian refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. The alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> began over a century of cooperation, and the strengthened state passed to the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Traidenis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Traidenis</b> and later Vytenis the state regained strength, and around 1316 Vytenis's brother Gediminas, who gave his name to the Gediminid dynasty, succeeded to a grand duchy that was ready to expand into the lands of Rus' weakened by the Mongol invasions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Aizkraukle in present-day Latvia, the Grand Duchy of Lithuania under <b>Traidenis</b> defeated the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order, killing 71 knights, including the grand master, Ernst von Rassburg.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-5">5 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
